OTLP/OTAP gRPC receivers: `max_connection_age` and `max_connection_age_grace`
(gRPC's MAX_CONNECTION_AGE / MAX_CONNECTION_AGE_GRACE), off by default.

gRPC clients re-resolve the server's name only when a connection closes, so
behind a DNS name that fans out to several receivers (a headless Service with
`dns:///` + round_robin agents), a receiver added by a scale-up gets no
traffic until the clients restart. With an age set, each connection is sent a
graceful GOAWAY at its age (jittered +/-10% per connection); the client moves
new RPCs to a new connection (re-resolving) and the in-flight ones finish.
The grace bounds how long they may take; after it the connection is closed.

Config wiring over tonic's own `Server::max_connection_age(_grace)`. Needs
the patched tonic (patches/tonic-0001, via otap-rs's [patch.crates-io]):
tonic 0.14.6 sends no GOAWAY when a grace is set, panics the connection
task when there is none, and has no jitter.

- otap: `GrpcServerSettings` gains the two humantime fields (serde default
  `None`), `validate_connection_age` (grace needs an age, age > 0), and
  `common::apply_connection_age`, which `apply_server_tuning` applies.
- OTLP receiver: validated in `from_config`, plus a warning when grace <=
  `timeout` (an RPC cut by the grace gets no response, so it is resent).
- OTAP receiver: the same two config fields, validated, applied to its
  server builder.
- No connection counters: tonic reports neither the GOAWAY nor the forced
  close (it logs both at debug).
- Tests: validation; through `apply_server_tuning` on a real listener, a
  raw HTTP/2 client sees the two-step GOAWAY at 0.9-1.1 x the age (with and
  without a grace) and nothing without an age; an RPC in flight at the
  GOAWAY completes within the grace and one longer than the grace is cut at
  age + grace. (The GOAWAY/grace/jitter mechanics are tested in tonic.)

Not proposed upstream (otap-s3pq local patch).

